        "title": "Interrupts and Timers - NVIC, ISR Discipline, Latency, PWM",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Interrupts are how hardware steals the CPU. The core is running your main loop;\na byte arrives on the UART, a timer expires, a pin changes — and the hardware\n*forces* the core to drop everything, jump to a handler, and return. This is\nthe embedded concurrency primitive: not threads, but asynchronous hardware\nevents that preempt mainline code. Timers are the most important interrupt\nsource because they manufacture *time* — periodic ticks, deadlines, PWM edges."
    }
